---
interface Props {
  compact?: boolean;
}
const { compact = false } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="bg-primary-50 border border-primary-100 rounded-xl p-5 md:p-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto mb-10 text-left">
  <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-primary-700 uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Federal rules as of July 4, 2025</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">OBBBA changed the clock and the cap — not the rest of §1202</h3>
  <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-3">
    {compact
      ? 'Stock acquired on or before July 4, 2025 still needs a 5-year hold and a $10 million cap (or 10× basis). Stock acquired after that date uses a 3/4/5-year ladder (50/75/100%) and a $15 million cap, with a $75 million gross-asset test. Both the cap and the asset test are indexed after 2026.'
      : 'P.L. 119-21 §70431 (signed July 4, 2025) changed IRC §1202 for stock issued after that date. Stock acquired on or before July 4, 2025 still needs a 5-year hold and a $10 million per-issuer cap (or 10× basis). Stock acquired after uses a 3-year / 4-year / 5-year ladder (50% / 75% / 100%) and a $15 million cap, with the gross-asset ceiling raised from $50 million to $75 million. The cap and asset test are indexed after 2026.'}
  </p>
  {!compact && (
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-3">
      What did not change: C-corporation, original issuance, the active-business test, and the excluded industries. QSBS is still a Clinton (1993) / Obama (2010) / Trump (2025) statute — not a one-president giveaway.
    </p>
  )}
  <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
    JCT scores the expansion as a small near-term revenue <em>gain</em> through 2028, then −$17.2B over 2025–34
    (<a href="https://www.jct.gov/publications/2025/jcx-35-25/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline hover:text-gray-700">JCX-35-25</a>).
    That is a stock-and-flow number, not a $42 billion annual giveaway.
  </p>
</div>
